# FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

This phase is complete only when:

- The running application really uses its configured LLM for natural-language understanding.
- The application can interpret paraphrases that were never added as hardcoded rules.
- Conversational messages receive natural answers without unsolicited diagnostics.
- Tool selection is based on the understood request and actual capability availability.
- Context and follow-up references are used intelligently.
- All tool execution remains validated and authorized.
- Actual tool evidence controls the assistant's technical claims.
- The interface never converts missing evidence into a success indicator.
- The existing product's functionality and security remain intact.
- The actual build and regression tests have been run and honestly reported.

**Final instruction:** Stop extending the phrase dictionary as the primary solution. Wire the real configured AI model into the existing conversational brain, make the brain responsible for understanding the user's intent, keep execution in the trusted backend, and make the whole application behave like one coherent AI infrastructure assistant.

What is this?

My recommendation: make this the next development phase before adding any new diagnostic capabilities. More scripts will expand what EJII can do, but connecting the actual LLM to the conversation-and-tool lifecycle is what can make it feel like an intelligent assistant instead of a command parser.